# Copy to .env for local development.

# --- Server-side, and genuinely secret -------------------------------------
# These are read by the Netlify functions at request time. They have no VITE_
# prefix on purpose: Vite inlines any VITE_* variable into the browser bundle,
# which would publish them. `netlify dev` picks them up from this file; plain
# `vite dev` serves no /api/* routes at all, so use mock mode there instead.

# The fal.ai key this deployment generates with. Images, video and captions all
# run on it, and visitors supply no key of their own — which means every
# generation is billed to whoever sets this.
#
# Anyone who can sign in can spend it: a verified session is the whole of the
# check, here and on the ElevenLabs key below. Narrow *who can sign in* in Auth0
# if that is not what you want — the connection's settings, or a Login Action —
# rather than anywhere in this repository.
# FAL_KEY=

# The Anthropic key the "Idea" tab and both "Improve with AI" buttons generate
# with, calling the Claude API directly rather than through fal. Same deal as
# FAL_KEY: visitors supply no key of their own, so all of it is billed to
# whoever sets this.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The ElevenLabs key this deployment speaks with. It pays for changing a
# recorded voice into another one, and for fixing a clip that mispronounces its
# line: copying the voice out of the clip, then saying the line again properly.
# Billed by the character, roughly $0.10 per 1,000 on the multilingual model —
# a ten-second line is about 2 cents.
#
# There is no key field in the app any more, so this is the only one: without it
# the voice features are unavailable and say so. A copy left in someone's browser
# from the days when there was a field is deleted on their next visit.
#
# The proxy only forwards the handful of endpoints this app calls, refuses to
# delete any voice it did not create, and clears away its own abandoned clones
# when the account runs out of voice slots. See netlify/lib/elevenlabs.ts.
#
# Three things ElevenLabs lets you configure on a key, and what to do with them:
#  - Scope restriction: worth using, and it needs all five of text to speech,
#    speech to speech, voices read, voices write (this app creates and deletes
#    its own throwaway clones) and models read. Scope it tighter and cloning or
#    its cleanup starts failing.
#  - Credit quota: worth setting. It is the only hard ceiling on what a bad
#    afternoon can cost, and nothing in this app can spend past it.
#  - IP allowlisting: do NOT use it. These requests come from Netlify functions,
#    whose egress addresses are neither fixed nor published, so an allowlist
#    turns every voice request into a 403.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# There is no SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET here any more, and setting one does nothing.
# Supabase used to reject Auth0's tokens outright — row-level security only
# trusted JWTs signed with the project's own key — so `/api/session` verified an
# Auth0 token and signed a Supabase-shaped one to replace it. The project now
# registers Auth0 as a third-party auth provider and validates its tokens against
# the tenant's own published keys, so nothing signs anything and no secret is
# involved. Remove it from any deployed environment: it is a live credential that
# can mint a session as anybody, and nothing reads it.
#
# Nothing server-side talks to Supabase either, which is why SUPABASE_URL is gone
# with it. The browser reaches the project directly, under VITE_SUPABASE_URL
# below.

# Which tenant and which API to verify callers against. Both fall back to their
# VITE_ forms — the same public strings — so these are only worth setting to
# point the functions somewhere else. Tokens are verified locally against the
# tenant's published signing keys, so this costs no round trip per request.
#
# This pair is now what stands between an anonymous request and the fal key:
# /api/fal/* verifies the caller's Auth0 access token against these, where it
# used to verify a session this site had signed. Without either half it refuses
# every request rather than running open.
# AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api


# OPTIONAL, and only for the report bubble in the corner of the editor. Without
# both, /api/github/status answers "not configured" and the bubble says filing is
# not set up here, rather than taking a report someone has written to nowhere.
#
# The token wants to be a *fine-grained* personal access token, scoped to this
# one repository, with Issues: read and write and nothing else. That is the whole
# permission it needs, and so scoped it can do nothing else if it leaks: it
# cannot read code, cannot push, and cannot touch another repository. No VITE_
# prefix, for the usual reason — that would publish it in the bundle.
#
# Filing writes to a public tracker under this deployment's account, so
# /api/github/issues verifies the caller's Auth0 token exactly as /api/fal/* does
# and rate limits per account. The reporter's email address is attached from that
# verified token, never from the request body — which needs the tenant to add a
# https://editor-cat/email claim to its access tokens, or the issue carries the
# Auth0 account id instead. See the README.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_REPO=owner/repo

# Skips the session check on /api/fal/* entirely, for running `netlify dev`
# against a checkout with no Auth0 tenant. Never set this on a deployed site: it
# leaves the endpoint open for anyone who finds the URL to generate video on your
# account.
# FAL_PROXY_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS=1

# Cloudflare R2, where media lives. Two buckets, because they are reached in two
# quite different ways.
#
# The PUBLIC bucket holds published feed videos. Bind a Cloudflare custom domain
# to it — that is what puts the CDN in front, and what VITE_R2_PUBLIC_BASE below
# points at. Nothing signs a read: the objects are public and immutable, so a
# Cache Rule on `/v1/*` with a one-year edge and browser TTL serves every view
# from the edge for no egress. That is the whole reason for the move, and it is
# why Cloudflare Stream is not used: its per-delivered-minute price is around a
# hundred times R2's at any view volume worth having.
#
# The PRIVATE bucket holds generated media and word-shelf takes. No domain, no
# public read; every fetch is a short-lived presigned GET. It is not served
# through the CDN and does not want to be — those URLs are unique per issue, so
# they would miss cache every time, and the files are fetched once per device
# and then live in IndexedDB.
#
# CORS has to be set on both, and it is the thing most likely to be wrong in a
# way nothing reports: a progressive `<video src>` is not a CORS request, but
# hls.js fetches manifests and segments through XHR, so the feed fails only
# under HLS and only cross-origin. Allow PUT from the editor's origin, GET and
# HEAD with Range from the feed's, and expose Content-Length, Content-Range,
# ETag and Accept-Ranges.
#
# Scope the API token to these two buckets with Object Read & Write — the same
# discipline as the ElevenLabs key above. It needs delete, for taking a
# published video down. Never scope it account-wide.
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET_PUBLIC=editor-cat-media
# R2_BUCKET_PRIVATE=editor-cat-private

# Mintspace's Supabase project, named again here for the server side.
#
# Publishing writes into the public bucket under the *Mintspace* account's id,
# not this app's Auth0 subject, and /api/r2 verifies a Mintspace token to find
# out what that id is. The two identities are genuinely different people — see
# src/lib/mintspace/client.ts — and keying the files by the wrong one is how a
# delete comes to remove the feed row, find nothing under the prefix it derived,
# and report success over files nobody can reach again.
#
# Not a secret, and it falls back to VITE_MINTSPACE_SUPABASE_URL below, which
# names the same project. Set this only if the two ever need to differ.
# MINTSPACE_SUPABASE_URL=

# Only for a legacy Supabase project that still signs its tokens with a shared
# secret (HS256). New projects publish asymmetric keys at
# /auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json and need nothing here. This one IS a live
# credential — it can mint a session as anybody — so it has no VITE_ form and
# must never be given one.
# MINTSPACE_SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# --- Build-time, and not secret --------------------------------------------
# These ship in the bundle by design. All optional.

# Fakes every provider call locally, so no keys and no network are needed.
# VITE_MOCK_PROVIDERS=1

# REQUIRED for sign-in.
# The Auth0 tenant, the SPA application's client id, and the API identifier its
# access tokens are minted for. None is a secret: all three are visible in every
# authorisation request the browser makes.
#
# In Auth0, the application's Allowed Callback URLs, Allowed Logout URLs and
# Allowed Web Origins have to cover wherever this is served from. Unlike Google,
# Auth0 takes a wildcard subdomain — https://*.staging.example.com — which is
# what makes deploy previews work without an entry each. Only ever for a domain
# you own; never a shared one like *.netlify.app, where anyone can deploy a site
# that matches and collect your users' tokens.
# VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# VITE_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
# VITE_AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api

# Supabase project, where timelines are saved. Set both to enable cloud
# projects; leave both unset to run purely against this browser's storage.
# The anon key is public by design — row-level security is what protects data.
#
# Two things that cannot be set from here have to be done in a dashboard before
# a signed-in browser can read anything: the project must have Auth0 registered
# under Authentication → Third-Party Auth, and the tenant must run a Login Action
# adding `role: authenticated` to the ID token. Neither is visible to this file
# or to the bundle, and getting either wrong looks like an empty project list
# rather than an error. See the README.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# Mintspace, the video feed the export dialog can publish to. Set both to offer
# "Publish to Mintspace" alongside the download; leave them unset and the dialog
# only downloads, with no mention of it.
#
# This is Mintspace's Supabase project — the one its own schema.sql was run
# against. It may be the same project as the pair above, since Mintspace keeps
# everything it owns under a `mintspace` schema and a bucket of the same name,
# or a completely separate one. Either way the anon key is public by design.
#
# Publishing signs in to a *Mintspace* account, which is not the Auth0 account
# this editor signs in with: Mintspace's row level security is built on Supabase
# Auth, and `auth.uid()` cannot so much as parse an Auth0 subject. So the export
# dialog asks for that account separately, and remembers it.
# VITE_MINTSPACE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# VITE_MINTSPACE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# Optional, and only cosmetic: where Mintspace is served from, so a published
# video can be linked. Without it the success message links the uploaded file,
# which is public and plays on its own.
# VITE_MINTSPACE_URL=https://mintspace.example.com

# Where published videos are served from: the Cloudflare custom domain bound to
# the public R2 bucket. This is the origin every feed URL is built on, so it is
# in the page already and is not a secret.
#
# Unset means the R2 publish path is off and the editor says so, rather than
# building URLs against nothing. It is also what /api/media reads to decide
# whether this deployment's own CDN is a host it may proxy for.
# VITE_R2_PUBLIC_BASE=https://cdn.example.com
